{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>וַיַּעֲבֹר אַבְרָם וְגוֹ׳</b> The verse informs us that Abraham was passing through the land from place to place, and not one of the people of the land said a word to him. And even though the masters of the land — the Canaanites — were at that time in the land through which Abraham passed, no one opened his mouth or chirped." "<b>Further,</b> it alludes that Abraham was passing through and did not hold himself in importance but rather as an עברי (Hebrew), along the lines of what it says (Genesis 14:13) ״אַבְרָם הָעִבְרִי״. And the reason it says <b>וְהַכְּנַעֲנִי</b> etc. — and did not suffice with what it said in the preceding verse, ״אַרְצָה כְּנַעַן״ — is that here it informed of the time when he entered the jurisdiction of the Canaanite, whereas what it said earlier, ״אַרְצָה כְּנַעַן״, is no proof, for it called it that after the name of the future." "<b>Or</b> it is possible that the name Canaan alludes, in the manner that our Sages of blessed memory (Yalkut) expounded in the portion of Shelach etc.: ״אֶרֶץ כְּנַעַן״ — it alluded to Israel, who descended from Abraham at one hundred years old and Sarah at ninety years old, which are hinted at in the numerical calculation of the word כנען. For this reason it was necessary to inform <b>וְהַכְּנַעֲנִי אָז בָּאָרֶץ</b>." "<b>Further,</b> the informing of <b>וְהַכְּנַעֲנִי</b> may intend to indicate the opening of acquisition in the land, for the Canaanite was then in the land — and he is a slave, as it is written (Genesis 9:25) ״עֶבֶד עֲבָדִים יִהְיֶה לְאֶחָיו״; he has no possession, and anyone who seizes his possessions from the children of Shem or Japheth will acquire them and their soil. And the Holy One, blessed be He, came and awarded the matter to Abraham. And without this, had the Canaanite not been in the land but rather the children of Japheth, there would have been no grounds to remove it from their hands once they had acquired it. And our Sages of blessed memory said (Torat Kohanim, parashat Kedoshim) that it belonged to the children of Shem, and Abraham, of the children of Shem, had acquired it previously. And according to their reasoning, HaShem informed that he did not acquire it by virtue of being a Canaanite — the hand of a slave being as the hand of his master." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend, given that HaShem wished to say to Abram ״לְזַרְעֲךָ אֶתֵּן אֶת הָאָרֶץ״, and until now I would have known that the land belonged to Shem, and Abraham is of his descendants, and it is not known that it belonged to Canaan at that time — and a person might say: what is HaShem giving him, something that has been his from of old and from beforehand? And although it was previously assigned to Canaan, I have already explained that this is no proof. Therefore HaShem informed <b>וְהַכְּנַעֲנִי אָז בָּאָרֶץ</b>, and for this reason HaShem needed to promise him that He would give it to his seed."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {3 "In the phrase 'the hand of a slave being as the hand of his master,' the meaning is that a slave's property legally belongs to his master; thus Abraham acquired the land not as a Canaanite (slave) but as a son of Shem."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:40:47.455541408Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}